. 	ag{20} \] The last follows from (15), \(\sqrt3<17321/10000\), and \(\pi<22/7\). The first uses 65 terms of the alternating Catalan series and \(\pi>31415/10000\); the second is already enclosed by the real-tail certificate. Their exact combination is (19). Markov's inequality on the fixed interval \([-1,2]\) costs only a power of \(N\) for each derivative, because the ordinary polynomial \(F_N\) has degree at most \(N-1\). The inequality applies to complex coefficients as well: at the point being estimated, rotate the derivative to the positive real axis and apply the real polynomial inequality to that real part. \(\square\) The [geometric action certificate](../repro/results/research-three-crossing-closing-action.json) gives \(\Re W(0)>1537/1000\). Thus (19) is a strict off-center gap at the actual geometric rate, independently of whether that rate has already been proved to contribute. The exact preparation error is also small on the entire real period. Lemmas 1--2 give \(\|\mathbf e(w)\|_2\le \operatorname{poly}(N)e^{Nb_2}\) uniformly for \(-2\le w\le2\). Hence the original decomposition \(\alpha=\kappa^3A_Nh+\delta_N\) satisfies \[ |\mathbf e(w)^TJM^3J\delta_N| \le \|\mathbf e(w)\|_2\|M^2\|_2\|M\|_2\|\delta_N\|_2 \le \operatorname{poly}(N)e^{N(2b_2+c)}, \qquad 2b_2+c< rac32, 	ag{21} \] with \(c\) the previously proved one-crossing norm exponent. Coordinate projections do not change this estimate. Markov's inequality retains all fixed ordinary derivatives on the same real interval. ## 4. A smaller Newton cutoff for intermediate angles For \(u\ge3/5\) and \(y\ge13/40\), Lemma 2 implies \[ V_u(y)\le V_{3/5}(13/40)< rac{249}{500}. 	ag{22} \] For \(y\le1/2\), this uses monotonicity in \(u\) and the fact that \(V_u\) decreases in \(y\) beyond its maximum \(y_u\le1/4\). For \(y\ge1/2\), it follows from \(V_u(y)\le0\). Consequently the part of the original source (17) with \(i/N\ge13/40\) has uniform upper rate, for \(3/5\le|u|\le\pi\), \[  rac{249}{500}+ rac{729}{12